Radio Caprice - Hard Bop	Hard-bop - jazz style, developed from bebop and cool-jazz, incorporating elements of soul, church music (gospel) and blues. The peak of his popularity came in the 1950-60s of the XX century. Basically this period in jazz is associated with such personalities as Sonny Rollins, John Coltrane, Miles Davis, Art Blakey and Charles Mingus. Subsequently, the followers of bebop and hard-bop added a little more style to it in the form of modal jazz, in which the harmony of the structure of individual parts became even more free, but this was usually manifested only when playing chords on the piano (including low tones). Thanks to this jazz period, instrumentalists were able to improvise with different scales of the scale. At about the same time that cool jazz took root on the West Coast, jazz musicians from Detroit, Philadelphia and New York began to develop more solid, heavy variations of the old formula of bebop, known as Hard Bop or Firm Bebop. Closely resembling traditional bebop in its aggressiveness and technic	Jazz-Classical	Russian Federation	Russian	http://www.radcap.ru/play/rc3/radio+caprice-hard+bop.pls	http://79.111.14.76:9047/;	http://79.111.14.76:8000/hardbop	-	-	-
