Radio Caprice - Mainstream jazz	http://www.radcap.ru/index-d.html Mainstream (eng. Mainstream) includes three currents: Stride (northeast jazz), Kansas City style and West Coast jazz. Hot stride reigned in Chicago, led by such masters as Louis Armstrong, Andy Condon, Jimmy Mac Partland. Kansas City is characterized by lyrical plays in blues style. West Coast Jazz developed in Los Angeles under the direction of Miles Davis, and later turned into cool jazz. After the end of the dominant fashion of large orchestras in the era of big bands, when the music of large orchestras on the stage began to crowd small jazz ensembles, swing music continued to sound. Many famous swing soloists, after concert performances in ball rooms, loved to play for fun on spontaneously arranged jams in small clubs on 52nd Street in New York. And it was not only those who worked as sidemen in large orchestras, such as Ben Webster, Coleman Hawkins, Lester Young, Roy Eldridge, Johnny Hodges, Buck Clayton, but also the big band leaders themselves - Duke Ellington, Count	Jazz-Classical	Russian Federation	Russian	http://www.radcap.ru/play/rc3/radio+caprice-mainstream+jazz.pls	http://79.111.14.76:9085/;	http://79.111.14.76:8000/mainstreamjazz	-	-	-
Radio Caprice - Symphony	http://www.radcap.ru/index-d.html  Symphony is a piece of music, usually in a cyclical sonata form, intended to be performed, as a rule, by a symphony orchestra; one of the most important genres of symphonic music. In addition to the orchestra, the chorus and vocals can be included in the symphony (there are cantata symphonies); symphonies can converge with other genres, for example, there are symphonies-suites, symphonies-rhapsodies, symphonies-fantasies, symphonies-ballads, symphonies-legends, symphonies-poems, symphonies-requiems, symphonies-ballets, symphonies-dramas (cantata genus) and theater symphonies as a form of opera. Initially, in ancient Greece, the “symphony” was called the euphonic sound of tones, joint singing in unison. In ancient Rome, the so-called ensemble, orchestra. In the Middle Ages, secular music was generally considered a “symphony” (in France, this value remained until the 18th century), some musical instruments (in particular, the wheel lyre) could be called that. The prototype	Classical	Russian Federation	Russian	http://www.radcap.ru/play/rc3/radio+caprice-symphonies.pls	http://79.111.14.76:9079/;	http://79.111.14.76:8000/symphony	-	-	-
Radio Caprice - Modern Classical	http://www.radcap.ru/index-d.html Modern (from the French. Moderne, - new, modern) - the name of the style, which was widely used in music of the late XIX beginning of the XX centuries, the main content of which was the desire of musicians to oppose their work to the historicism and eclecticism of the second half of the XIX century - hence the name  Therefore, the term "modern classical" should be distinguished from the general meaning of the word "modern" or "modern art", as well as the term "modernism", meaning all the most avant-garde, experimental and formalistic trends in the art of the 20th century. Some sources mistakenly draw a parallel with such a genre as "Classical Crossover", in which for the most part classical or "near classical" sounds music in modern processing.	Classical	Russian Federation	Russian	http://www.radcap.ru/play/rc2/radio+caprice-modern+classical.pls	http://79.120.39.202:9099/;	http://79.120.39.202:8000/modernclassical	-	-	-
