Radio Caprice - Cool / West coast  Jazz	http://www.radcap.ru/index-d.html   Kul-jazz (also “cool jazz” - literally translated from English. Cool jazz) is a style of modern jazz that emerged in the late 1940s on the West Coast of the United States, which became widespread mainly among white musicians, boppers, and reached its greatest prosperity in the 1960s years The word cool in relation to jazz arose after the release of the album “Birth of the Cool” (1949-1950 recording) by the famous jazz musician Miles Davis. The origin of the style and the authorship of the name are associated with the name of swinging saxophonist Lester Young, who, as far back as the 1930s, developed the “cold” style of sound extraction opposite to hot jazz. In terms of sound production and harmonic thinking, cool jazz has a lot in common with modal jazz. It is characterized by emotional restraint, a tendency to come closer to composer music (strengthening the role of composition, form and harmony, polyphony of texture), the introduction of symphonic orchestra instrument	Jazz-Classical	Russian Federation	Russian	http://www.radcap.ru/play/rc2/radio+caprice-cool+jazz.pls	http://79.120.39.202:9085/;	http://79.120.39.202:8000/cooljazz	-	-	-
